  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "sosevur",
      "phase": "rise",
      "source": "Tradition",
      "title": "Strike, Sala!",
      "native": "ಹೊಯ್ ಸಳ",
      "when": "c. 1026 to 1108 CE",
      "at": 8,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.2,
          13.3
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Tradition says a Jain teacher, Sudatta, was praying at Sosevur, in the western hills, when a tiger sprang. He called to his pupil, ‘Strike, Sala!’ (hoy, Sala), and Sala killed it. The story explains the name Hoysala, and the fight became the dynasty’s emblem. Inscriptions show them in the 11th century as hill chiefs under the Chalukyas.",
      "today": "Angadi, Chikkamagaluru district, Karnataka, is identified with Sosevur."
    },
    {
      "place": "talakad",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Victory at Talakad",
      "native": "ತಲಕಾಡು",
      "when": "1116 CE",
      "at": 30,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.8,
          12.8
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "belur",
          "to": "talakad",
          "traveller": "hoysala-army",
          "style": "hoysala",
          "bend": 0.12
        }
      ],
      "text": "Bittiga, better known as Vishnuvardhana, made the Hoysalas a power. In 1116 his Jain general Gangaraja drove the Cholas out of Talakad, their stronghold in Gangavadi, the old Ganga country by the Kaveri. Vishnuvardhana took the title ‘taker of Talakad’ (Talakadugonda). He still owed loyalty to the Chalukyas, but raided their lands too; his kingdom now reached the Kaveri.",
      "today": "Talakad, on the Kaveri, is now half buried by sand dunes."
    },
    {
      "place": "belur",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "The temple at Belur",
      "native": "ಚೆನ್ನಕೇಶವ ದೇವಾಲಯ",
      "when": "1117 CE",
      "at": 34,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.2,
          13.1
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "In 1117 Vishnuvardhana consecrated the Chennakeshava temple at Belur, by tradition to mark his victories. Its soft soapstone is carved as finely as sandalwood: friezes of elephants and epics, and dozens of bracket figures of dancers (madanikas). Tradition says he turned from Jainism to the Vaishnavism of the teacher Ramanuja; historians debate this, and his queen Shantala stayed Jain.",
      "today": "The temple is still in worship, in Hassan district, Karnataka."
    },
    {
      "place": "halebidu",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Dorasamudra",
      "native": "ದ್ವಾರಸಮುದ್ರ",
      "when": "c. 1121 to 1160 CE",
      "at": 38,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.4,
          13.1
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The capital, Dorasamudra, ‘gateway to the sea’, was named after its great tank. About 1121 an official, Ketamalla, began the Hoysaleshwara temple there: twin shrines to Shiva, named for the king and Queen Shantala. Thousands of figures cover its walls. Jain temples (basadis) rose nearby; the court gave to both faiths. The name Halebidu, ‘old capital’, came after its fall.",
      "today": "Halebidu, Hassan district, Karnataka."
    },
    {
      "place": "lakkundi",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Ballala II breaks free",
      "native": "ಲಕ್ಕುಂಡಿ",
      "when": "c. 1187 to 1220 CE",
      "at": 53,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.2,
          14.3
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "halebidu",
          "to": "lakkundi",
          "traveller": "hoysala-army",
          "style": "hoysala",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "As the Chalukya kingdom broke up, Veera Ballala II fought the Yadavas of Devagiri for its lands. In 1190, his inscriptions say, he beat the Yadava Bhillama V at Soratur, and for a time he held court at Lokkigundi (Lakkundi) in the north. He took the full titles of an independent king. The Hoysalas now ruled most of southern Karnataka.",
      "today": "Lakkundi, Gadag district, keeps dozens of temples and step wells of this age."
    },
    {
      "place": "kannanur",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "Guardians of the Cholas",
      "native": "கண்ணனூர்",
      "lang": "ta",
      "when": "c. 1220 to 1262 CE",
      "at": 66,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.2,
          12.2
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "halebidu",
          "to": "kannanur",
          "traveller": "hoysala-army",
          "style": "hoysala",
          "bend": 0.12
        }
      ],
      "text": "The Cholas were failing. When the Pandyas sacked Thanjavur, and a chief named Kopperunjinga captured the Chola king Rajaraja III around 1231, Narasimha II sent armies to rescue him. His son Someshvara built a second capital at Kannanur near Srirangam and dominated the Kaveri delta. He was killed fighting the Pandya Jatavarman Sundara Pandya, about 1262.",
      "today": "Samayapuram, near Tiruchirappalli, stands on the site of Kannanur Koppam."
    },
    {
      "place": "somanathapura",
      "phase": "empire",
      "title": "The last great temple",
      "native": "ಸೋಮನಾಥಪುರ",
      "when": "1268 CE",
      "at": 75,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77,
          12.6
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "After Someshvara the kingdom was split between his sons, Narasimha III at Dorasamudra and Ramanatha in the Tamil lands, and they fought each other. In 1268 Narasimha’s general Somanatha founded Somanathapura and its Keshava temple: three shrines on a star-shaped plan, with sculptors’ names, such as Mallitamma, carved beneath their work. It is one of the best preserved Hoysala temples.",
      "today": "Somanathapura, Mysuru district; the temple is a protected monument, not in worship."
    },
    {
      "place": "halebidu",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "Malik Kafur at Dorasamudra",
      "native": "ದ್ವಾರಸಮುದ್ರ",
      "when": "1311 CE",
      "at": 88,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75.8,
          16.5
        ],
        "width": 13
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "devagiri",
          "to": "halebidu",
          "traveller": "kafur-army",
          "style": "delhi",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Ballala III, Narasimha’s son, had reunited the kingdom. In 1311 he was away in the south, hoping to profit from a Pandya civil war, when Malik Kafur, Alauddin Khalji’s general, marched from Devagiri. He hurried back and soon submitted, handing over treasure, elephants and horses. Persian sources say he guided Kafur on south; his son went to Delhi.",
      "today": "Halebidu’s temples, damaged in the raids, still stand."
    },
    {
      "place": "tiruvannamalai",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "Ballala III’s last fight",
      "native": "திருவண்ணாமலை",
      "lang": "ta",
      "when": "1327 to 1343 CE",
      "at": 93,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.9,
          12.4
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "tiruvannamalai",
          "to": "kannanur",
          "traveller": "hoysala-army",
          "style": "hoysala",
          "bend": 0.2
        }
      ],
      "text": "In 1327 another Delhi army sacked Dorasamudra, and Ballala III moved his court to Tiruvannamalai. When Madurai broke from Delhi as a sultanate, he fought it. In 1343 he besieged Kannanur, was captured and killed; Ibn Battuta, who reached Madurai soon after, says his stuffed skin hung on its wall. The Sangama brothers took over his lands.",
      "today": "Tiruvannamalai, Tamil Nadu, around the Arunachaleshvara temple."
    },
    {
      "place": "belur",
      "phase": "after",
      "title": "Sacred ensembles",
      "when": "1343 CE to today",
      "at": 99,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.5,
          13
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The Hoysala lands passed to Vijayanagara, whose founders may have served Ballala III; this is debated. The temples remained. By one estimate the Hoysalas built over 1,500 temples, and around a hundred survive. In 2023 UNESCO listed the Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas, at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura, as a World Heritage Site.",
      "today": "Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura are World Heritage Sites."
    }
  ],
