  "people": {
    "heading": "Kings and a queen",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "vishnuvardhana",
        "name": "Vishnuvardhana",
        "native": "ವಿಷ್ಣುವರ್ಧನ",
        "life": "r. c. 1108 to 1152",
        "role": "The king who made the Hoysalas a power",
        "steps": [
          1,
          2,
          3
        ],
        "text": "Born Bittiga (Bittideva), he took Gangavadi from the Cholas and pushed north against the Chalukyas. Tradition says the teacher Ramanuja, fleeing a Chola king, converted him to Vaishnavism, and that he took the name Vishnuvardhana then. Historians debate how far he turned from the Jain faith of his family, for he kept giving to Jain temples."
      },
      {
        "id": "shantala",
        "name": "Shantala Devi",
        "native": "ಶಾಂತಲಾ ದೇವಿ",
        "life": "died 1131",
        "role": "Queen, dancer and patron",
        "steps": [
          2,
          3
        ],
        "text": "Vishnuvardhana’s chief queen, praised in inscriptions as a dancer and musician. She was a Jain and gave to Jain temples at Shravanabelagola, where an inscription records her death in 1131. A legend says Belur’s dancing figures were modelled on her. Karnataka’s award for dance, the Shantala Natya Shri, is named after her."
      },
      {
        "id": "ballala-iii",
        "name": "Veera Ballala III",
        "native": "ವೀರ ಬಲ್ಲಾಳ",
        "life": "r. 1292 to 1343",
        "role": "The last great Hoysala king",
        "steps": [
          7,
          8
        ],
        "text": "He reunited his father’s and uncle’s lands, then faced Delhi. He submitted to Malik Kafur in 1311 and paid tribute, but kept his kingdom. After Dorasamudra was sacked in 1327 he ruled from Tiruvannamalai and fought the Madurai Sultanate. His death at Kannanur in 1343 ended Hoysala power, and Vijayanagara rose on his lands."
      }
    ]
  }
